.TH OPENSAVE 1 "July 2026" "OpenSave" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
opensave \- peer-to-peer game save sync
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B opensave
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.RB [ --json ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B OpenSave
keeps game saves in sync between your own machines. Devices talk directly to
each other over the LAN, or through a relay when they are on different
networks. There is no account, no server to sign up to and no storage quota;
the relay only forwards encrypted frames and never stores saves.
.PP
The command line tool is a complete client. It can detect saves, track them,
pair with other devices, sync, and run the background daemon, so a headless
machine \(em a server, or a Steam Deck that lives in Game Mode \(em never needs
the desktop application.
.PP
Commands that need live peer state talk to a running daemon over its local
HTTP API. Commands that only touch local data work whether or not a daemon is
running.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.B --json
Emit machine\-readable JSON instead of formatted text. Accepted by every
command, for use in scripts.
.SH GAMES
.TP
.BR scan " [" --all ]
Look for game saves on this machine: Steam libraries, Proton prefixes,
emulators, and thousands of titles from the Ludusavi manifest. Each result
shows how many files the folder holds, how big it is, and when it was last
written \(em which is what tells a save still in use from one an install left
behind.
.IP
Folders holding no files are hidden, since Steam creates one for every game
you own whether or not saves go there.
.B --all
lists them too, for tracking a folder before the game has first saved to it.
.IP
Results are listed one game at a time. A game found in more than one place has
its other folders indented beneath it, each labelled as part of the same save,
as already inside the folder above, or as another copy \(em usually one an
install left behind. Every folder still gets its own number, so
.B add
takes the one you are looking at; joining several to one game is
.BR "locations add" .
.IP
With
.BR --json ,
results come back in that same order \(em index
.I n
is what
.BI "add " n
tracks \(em each carrying its file count, size, last-written time, and the
group id and role saying which rows are one game and what each folder is.
.B measured
false means the folder could not be read, which is not the same as empty.
.TP
.BI "add " "name path"
Track a save folder or file.
.TP
.BI "remove " gameId
Stop tracking a game. Save files and existing snapshots are left on disk.
.TP
.B status
Show tracked games, their branches, and paired devices.
.TP
.BI "add " path
Track a folder or file named from its path: the game's own folder rather than
.I Saves
or a Steam account's number, and a Nintendo Switch save by the name its
emulator shows.
.TP
.B untrack-all --yes
Stop tracking every game at once. Snapshots are kept; scanning again adds the
games back from where they are now.
.TP
.BI "launch " gameId
Start the game with its launch program.
.TP
.BI "wrap " gameId " -- " command
Run a game through OpenSave: the newest save from your other devices first,
then the command, and a snapshot and a sync when it exits. For a Steam launch
option, or anything else that starts a game.
.TP
.BR sessions " [" \fIgameId\fR ]
When games were played on this device, and for how long.
.TP
.BR activity " [" \fIgameId\fR "] [" "--limit \fIN\fR" ]
What happened to each save lately, newest first and by day: what came from
your other devices and what went to them, snapshots, play, deletions and
restores \(em the app's Activity timeline.
.TP
.B collection list|create|rename|delete|add|remove
Group games into collections.
.I Favourites
is built in.
.SH SYNC
.TP
.BI "sync [" gameId "|--all]"
Sync now. With no argument, syncs every tracked game. Says what each game did,
or why it did not sync: syncing is paused, no other device is online, or the
game is held back because its save was emptied here.
.TP
.BR pause " [" \fIduration\fR ]
Stop syncing for a while (30m, 1h, 3h and so on) or, with no duration, until
.BR resume .
Snapshots are still taken.
.TP
.B resume
Start syncing again, and catch up on what changed while paused.
.TP
.B transfers
What is moving between this device and others now, and what moved lately.
.TP
.B peers
List paired devices, devices discovered on this network, and pending pairing
requests.
.TP
.BI "pair " "host[:port]"
Ask a device on the LAN to pair. Pairing is mutual: it must be approved on the
other device.
.TP
.B pair requests
Show incoming pairing requests.
.TP
.BI "pair approve " peerId
Approve an incoming pairing request.
.TP
.BI "pair reject " peerId
Reject an incoming pairing request.
.TP
.BI "peers games " peerId
What that device is tracking.
.TP
.BI "probe " "host[:port]"
Check whether a device answers, before pairing with it.
.TP
.BI "forget " peerId
Remove a stale device record.
.TP
.BR conflicts " [" --locations ]
Saves that changed on two devices at once and wait on a decision, with what
differs; then any of a game's extra save folders that diverged the same way.
With
.BR --json ,
the games keyed by id;
.B --locations --json
gives the folders instead.
.TP
.BI "resolve " "gameId choice" " [--location " folder ]
Settle a conflict:
.B keep-both
(the other device's save goes on a branch),
.BR keep-local " or " keep-remote .
With
.BR --location ,
settles one of the game's extra save folders instead, with
.BR keep-local " or " keep-remote ;
a game whose only conflict is in one folder needs no
.BR --location .
.TP
.B offers
Games your other devices sync that this device was set to ask about
.RB ( "config set unknown-game-from-peer ask" ),
waiting for a folder here.
.TP
.BI "offers place " "gameId folder"
Track an offered game here, in that folder.
.TP
.BI "offers decline " gameId
Decline it; it is not offered again unless you track it yourself.
.TP
.BI "unpair " peerId
Drop a paired device.
.TP
.B relay status|join|leave
Manage internet sync. Devices that share a relay room code find each other
across networks without port forwarding.
.SH HISTORY
.TP
.BI "snapshot " "gameId " [ comment ]
Create a snapshot of the current save.
.TP
.BI "snapshots " gameId
List snapshots on the active branch, newest first.
.TP
.BI "rollback " "gameId snapshotId"
Restore a snapshot.
.TP
.BI "branch " "gameId name" " [--empty]"
Create a branch, for keeping parallel playthroughs. It starts from your
current save, so switching to it changes nothing until you play.
.B --empty
starts it with no save instead, for a fresh run \(em switching to that branch
clears the save folder.
.TP
.BI "checkout " "gameId name"
Switch to a branch. Your current save is snapshotted onto the branch you are
leaving first, so the switch can always be undone by switching back; if that
snapshot cannot be taken, nothing is changed.
.TP
.BI "export " "gameId directory"
Copy the current save out to a folder, exactly as the game wrote it \(em no
archive and no proprietary wrapper.
.TP
.BI "snapshot --all " [ comment ]
Snapshot every tracked game.
.TP
.BI "snapshot-diff " "gameId from to"
What changed from one snapshot to another, file by file.
.TP
.BI "branch-delete " "gameId name"
Delete a branch and its snapshots.
.TP
.BI "snapshot-delete " "gameId snapshotId"
Delete one snapshot.
.TP
.BI "snapshot-pin " "gameId snapshotId"
Keep a snapshot through every limit and clean-up;
.B snapshot-unpin
lets the limits apply to it again.
.TP
.BI "snapshot-note " "gameId snapshotId" " [" note ]
Write a note on a snapshot; with no note, remove it.
.TP
.BR prune " [" --apply-default ]
Apply the retention limits now.
.TP
.BR storage " [" --compact ]
Space taken per game, the biggest snapshots, and what pruning would free.
.B --compact
has older snapshots share the files they have in common now, rather than in
the background.
.TP
.BR verify " [" \fIgameId\fR "] [" --repair | --remove-damaged ]
Read every snapshot back and check each file against its checksum.
.B --repair
puts back damaged ones from their cloud copies;
.B --remove-damaged
removes from the history what cannot be restored.
.TP
.BR emptied " [" "\fIgameId\fR delete|restore" ]
Games whose save files were all deleted here, held back from your other
devices until you say whether that was meant:
.B restore
puts them back,
.B delete
lets the deletion reach your other devices.
.TP
.BI "files " "gameId snapshotId" " [" path ]
List a snapshot's contents, or restore one file from it.
.TP
.BI "backup export " file.sscb
Write every save into one portable backup archive;
.B backup import
reads one back.
.SH CLOUD BACKUP
.TP
.BI "cloud connect " provider
Sign in to Google Drive, Dropbox or OneDrive.
.TP
.BI "cloud setup " provider
Configure WebDAV, a webhook, or a local folder.
.TP
.B cloud status
The provider, and whether it is connected;
.B cloud disconnect
forgets it.
.TP
.B cloud browse
Everything stored in the cloud;
.BI "cloud list " gameId
one game's snapshots there.
.TP
.BI "cloud push " gameId
Upload a game's local snapshots.
.TP
.BI "cloud restore " "gameId file"
Bring one cloud snapshot back;
.BI "cloud delete " "gameId file"
removes one, and
.BI "cloud delete " gameId " --yes"
every cloud copy of a game.
.TP
.B cloud check
Newer saves your other devices left in the cloud;
.BR "cloud take" | skip " \fIgameId\fR"
brings one here or keeps this device's.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.TP
.B config [list]
Show current settings.
.TP
.BI "config set " "key value"
Change a setting. Keys:
.BR device-name ,
.BR match-by-app-id ,
.BR port ,
.BR snapshot-limit ,
.BR manual-snapshot-limit ,
.BR relay-url ,
.BR update-channel .
.IP
.B port
is the local API and peer-to-peer port, 8383 by default; it takes effect the
next time the daemon starts.
.IP
.B update-channel
is
.B stable
(published releases only, the default) or
.B beta
(pre\-releases as well). A build that is itself a pre\-release follows the beta
channel whatever this is set to, since GitHub's "latest release" skips
pre\-releases and such a build would otherwise report itself up to date until
the final release overtook it. The stable release is still offered as soon as
it is the newer of the two, so neither direction is a one\-way door.
.TP
.BI "game " "gameId set key value"
Change one game's settings. Keys:
.BR name ,
.BR path ,
.BR app-id ,
.BR exe-path ,
.BR cover-url ,
.BR auto-sync ,
.BR max-snapshots ,
.BR max-manual-snapshots .
.IP
.B max-snapshots
caps the snapshots taken automatically as the game saves;
.B max-manual-snapshots
caps the ones you took yourself, and defaults to 0, meaning they are kept
forever. The two are counted separately so a game that autosaves often cannot
push out a snapshot you took on purpose.
.TP
.B scanpath list|add|remove
Extra folders to scan, for libraries in places the scan does not look.
.TP
.B exclude list|add|remove
Manage folders that scans skip, for stale save locations you do not want
offered again.
.TP
.BI "link " "gameId otherGameId"
Treat two tracked games as the same game, for a title tracked under different
names on different machines. The second is merged in and removed from the
library; its save files are untouched.
.TP
.BI "unlink " aliasGameId
Undo a link, restoring the merged game as its own entry.
.TP
.BI "links " gameId
Show the ids linked to a game.
.TP
.BI "ignore " gameId
List the files excluded from syncing for a game.
.TP
.BI "ignore " "gameId add|remove pattern..."
Stop, or resume, syncing files matching a pattern. Patterns are written like
a .gitignore: a bare name matches at any depth, a leading / anchors to the top
of the save folder, a trailing / means a folder and its contents, and a leading
! re-includes something an earlier line excluded. Matching ignores case.
.IP
These rules decide what SYNCS and nothing else \(em excluded files are still
captured in snapshots and restored by rollbacks, so an exclusion can never be
the thing that loses one. Each device applies its own list; set the same
patterns on both.
.TP
.BI "ignore " "gameId clear"
Remove every pattern.
.TP
.BI "ignore " "gameId test path..."
Report whether each path would sync under the current rules.
.TP
.BI "locations " gameId
List a game's save locations \(em its main folder plus any extra ones.
.TP
.BI "locations add " "gameId name path"
Add another folder to a game, for a title whose save is split across more than
one place: the data in one folder, settings or mods in another. Use the SAME
.I name
on your other devices \(em the name is what the two sides match on, since the
folder lives somewhere different on each machine. A folder that is the game's
main save, or inside it, is refused: its files are covered already, and two
locations describing the same files fight over them.
.TP
.BI "locations remove " "gameId name"
Stop covering one. Its files are left exactly where they are.
.SH SERVICE
.TP
.BR "daemon start " [ --port " " \fIN\fR | --port " " auto ]
Run the daemon in the foreground: REST API, save watcher and peer sync.
Without
.B --port
it uses the configured port, 8383 unless changed.
.B "--port auto"
takes whatever the system has free, which is the quick way past a clash; the
port it settles on is written to
.I ~/.opensave/daemon.addr
so the CLI still finds it. To move off 8383 for good, use
.BR "config set port" .
.TP
.B daemon status
Report whether a daemon is running and where.
.TP
.B daemon stop
Stop a daemon started by
.BR "daemon start" .
Daemons belonging to the desktop application or to systemd are deliberately
left alone.
.TP
.B service install|uninstall|status
Manage a
.B systemd --user
unit so syncing runs without anyone opening an application. Linux only.
.TP
.B completion bash|zsh|fish
Print a shell completion script.
.TP
.BI "upnp " port " " [ --delete ]
Forward, or remove, a router port via UPnP.
.TP
.BR update " [" --check ]
Replace this binary with the newest release for its channel, or with
.B --check
only report whether one exists. See
.B config set update-channel
for which releases that is; a headless install has no update banner to click,
which is the whole reason this exists.
.TP
.B version
Print the version.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.opensave/opensave.db
Tracked games, settings, peers and snapshot metadata.
.TP
.I ~/.opensave/backups/
Snapshot archives.
.TP
.I ~/.opensave/daemon.addr
Address of the running daemon. Written on start, removed on shutdown; this is
how the CLI and the Steam Deck plugin find a daemon whose port may not be the
configured one.
.TP
.I ~/.opensave/daemon.pid
Process id, written only by
.BR "opensave daemon start" .
.TP
.I ~/.config/systemd/user/opensave-daemon.service
Installed by
.BR "opensave service install" .
.SH EXAMPLES
Track everything found on this machine, then sync it:
.PP
.nf
.RS
opensave scan
opensave add "Elden Ring" ~/.local/share/EldenRing
opensave daemon start &
opensave pair 192.168.1.42
opensave sync --all
.RE
.fi
.PP
Set up a Steam Deck to sync in the background, permanently:
.PP
.nf
.RS
opensave service install
systemctl --user enable --now opensave-daemon
sudo loginctl enable-linger $USER
.RE
.fi
.PP
Join two machines that are not on the same network:
.PP
.nf
.RS
opensave relay join my-shared-room-code    # on each device
opensave peers
.RE
.fi
.PP
Script against it:
.PP
.nf
.RS
opensave daemon status --json | jq .gameCount
opensave snapshots elden-ring --json | jq -r '.[0].id'
.RE
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on success and 1 on failure. With
.BR --json ,
failures print an object with an
.I error
field.
.B sync
also returns 1 when nothing could sync \(em paused, or no other device
online \(em so a script can tell a sync that happened from one that could not.
